  <section id="chart" aria-labelledby="chart-h">
    <h2 id="chart-h">Height Comparison Chart</h2>
    <p>This chart shows women and men from 4&nbsp;ft&nbsp;11&nbsp;in to 6&nbsp;ft&nbsp;6&nbsp;in, drawn at true scale. Use the tool above to put your own people side by side.</p>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/height-comparison-chart-3d.webp" width="1700" height="980" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Height comparison chart: 3D women and men from 4 ft 11 in to 6 ft 6 in at true scale"><figcaption>Women and men from 4&nbsp;ft&nbsp;11&nbsp;in to 6&nbsp;ft&nbsp;6&nbsp;in, with the U.S. averages marked.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section id="average" aria-labelledby="average-h">
    <h2 id="average-h">Average Height: How You Compare</h2>
    <p>The average U.S. adult man is <strong>5&nbsp;ft&nbsp;9&nbsp;in (175&nbsp;cm)</strong> and the average woman <strong>5&nbsp;ft&nbsp;3.5&nbsp;in (161&nbsp;cm)</strong> (NHANES 2021&ndash;2023). Height has barely changed in the U.S. for decades, while average weight has risen. For your exact percentile by sex and age, use the <a href="/height-percentile-calculator/">height percentile calculator</a>; to compare with other countries, see <a href="/average-height-by-country/">average height by country</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="differences" aria-labelledby="differences-h">
    <h2 id="differences-h">Common Height Differences</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Height differences at a glance</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Comparison</th><th scope="col">Difference</th><th scope="col">In cm</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 4 in vs 5 ft 10 in</th><td>6 in</td><td>15.2 cm</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 6 in vs 6 ft</th><td>6 in</td><td>15.2 cm</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 9 in vs 6 ft</th><td>3 in</td><td>7.6 cm</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 2 in vs 5 ft 8 in</th><td>6 in</td><td>15.2 cm</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft vs 6 ft</th><td>12 in</td><td>30.5 cm</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6 ft vs 6 ft 4 in</th><td>4 in</td><td>10.2 cm</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>A difference of 1 to 2&nbsp;inches is hard to notice unless people stand back to back. From about 3&nbsp;inches, eye levels clearly differ; at 6&nbsp;inches or more, the shorter person&rsquo;s eyes are around the taller person&rsquo;s chin or mouth.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="couples" aria-labelledby="couples-h">
    <h2 id="couples-h">Height Difference in Couples</h2>
    <p>Large studies of couples, including data from the UK Biobank, find the man is taller in over 90% of couples, and the average gap is close to the population gap of 5 to 6&nbsp;inches. People tend to pair with partners of similar relative height, so very tall women often have even taller partners. In surveys, many women say they prefer a partner several inches taller, while men tend to prefer a partner somewhat shorter than themselves.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to" aria-labelledby="how-to-h">
    <h2 id="how-to-h">How to Use the Height Comparison Tool</h2>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Choose units.</strong> Pick feet and inches or centimeters.</li><li><strong>Enter each person.</strong> Type a name, choose female or male, and enter the height. Add up to five people, or use a quick-add button for average heights.</li><li><strong>Read the chart.</strong> Everyone is drawn at true scale on a height grid, with the difference from the tallest person listed below.</li><li><strong>Share or save.</strong> Copy the link to share the same comparison, or download the chart as an image.</li></ol>
    <p>To compare weight and build as well as height, open the <a href="/body-visualizer/">3D body visualizer</a> and use Compare A/B.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="measure" aria-labelledby="measure-h">
    <h2 id="measure-h">How to Measure Your Height Accurately</h2>
    <p>Stand barefoot on a hard floor with your back to a wall, heels, buttocks and shoulders touching it, and look straight ahead. Place a flat object such as a book on top of your head, level with the floor, and mark the wall where it meets. Measure from the floor to the mark. Measure in the morning for your tallest reading; spinal discs compress slightly during the day. Need to convert the result? Use <a href="/cm-to-feet-and-inches/">cm to feet and inches</a> or <a href="/feet-to-cm/">feet to cm</a>.</p>
  </section>

